{"caoutchouc":{"id":"836abb80-374a-4e34-89f5-d13a81ac7b78","word":"caoutchouc","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"natural rubber","tags":["feminine"],"word":"borracha"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"natural rubber","tags":["masculine"],"word":"cautchu"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Many a passer-by thought Miss Arundel was listening to some subject of most touching interest: his Lordship was only detailing the benefit he derived one wet day from his caoutchouc cloak.","ref":"1831, L[etitia] E[lizabeth] L[andon], chapter XXIII, in Romance and Reality. […], volume I, London: Henry Colburn and Richard Bentley, […], →OCLC, page 299:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[171,181]],"id":"d3848a15-5df5-439b-be5b-ef5517849ce0"},{"text":"The Automaton can plainly, at the will of the casual spectator, speak any sentence or words, in any language, sing, whisper, laugh, &c.; the organs of articulation are formed of caoutchouc—exactly resembling those appertaing(sic) to humanity....","ref":"1846, editorial staff, The Speaking Automation in \"Scientific American\", series 1, Volume 2, Issue 13, page 98","id":"9f22b215-14a0-40ce-9634-c423c37e7d82"},{"text":"Resin, pitch, sealing-wax, caoutchouc, sulphur, gutta-percha, and generally all bodies commonly termed resinous, besides a great variety of others, will be found to acquire, after friction, the power of attracting light bodies, and hence will be said, according to a limitation of terms already accepted, to be in an electrized or electrified state .","ref":"1855, William Somerville Orr, Orr's Circle of the Sciences, page 205:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[27,37]],"id":"89f2b870-0270-46fc-86d1-b7a6435ee669"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms borrowed from French","English terms derived from French","English terms derived f